OPAL

Performance-first remote desktop for Linux, Apple Silicon macOS, and Windows.

Build

  make
  make verify
  make clean

The root Makefile detects the platform automatically. No platform-specific makefile or gmake command is required.

Linux

Fedora / Fedora Asahi:

  sudo dnf install -y gcc-c++ make git pkgconf-pkg-config SDL3-devel openssl-devel ffmpeg ffmpeg-devel pipewire pipewire-devel libportal libportal-devel xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

Arch / CachyOS:

  sudo pacman -S --needed base-devel git pkgconf sdl3 openssl ffmpeg pipewire pipewire-pulse libportal xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

Debian / Ubuntu:

  sudo apt-get install -y build-essential git pkg-config libsdl3-dev libssl-dev ffmpeg libavformat-dev libavcodec-dev libavutil-dev libswresample-dev libswscale-dev libpipewire-0.3-dev libportal-dev pipewire pipewire-pulse xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

Build and install:

  make -j"$(nproc)"
  sudo make install

macOS

Requirements: Apple Silicon, macOS 13+, Xcode Command Line Tools, Homebrew.

  xcode-select --install
  brew install pkg-config sdl3 openssl@3 ffmpeg git
  make install

Do not run sudo make install on macOS. OPAL builds/signs as the current user and invokes sudo only for the final copies into /usr/local.

Host permissions:

  System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Screen & System Audio Recording -> allow OPAL / opal
  System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Accessibility -> allow opal-input

Windows

Use an MSYS2 UCRT64 shell and install the UCRT64 C++ toolchain, pkgconf, SDL3, OpenSSL, and FFmpeg packages.

  make
  make install

The Windows build produces:

  build/opal.exe
  build/opal-input.exe

Windows host backends:

  capture   -> DXGI Desktop Duplication + D3D11
  encode    -> Media Foundation hardware H.264
  decode    -> FFmpeg D3D11VA
  present   -> direct D3D11 + SDL3 fallback
  input     -> SendInput
  clipboard -> CF_UNICODETEXT
  audio     -> WASAPI loopback + AAC
  service   -> interactive-user Task Scheduler task

Tailscale

Tailscale is optional but recommended for direct Tailnet connectivity.

Linux:

  curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh
  sudo tailscale up

macOS:

  brew install tailscale
  sudo brew services start tailscale
  sudo tailscale up

Check connectivity with:

  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

Run

  opal
  opal <connection-code>
  OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal <connection-code>
  opal doctor
  opal restart
  opal clean

On Linux Wayland, host setup can remember the selected xdg-desktop-portal screens and reuse the persistent PipeWire authorization on reconnect.

Architecture

Linux:

  capture   -> PipeWire + libportal
  input     -> /dev/uinput
  clipboard -> wl-clipboard / SDL3 fallback
  UDP       -> sendmmsg/recvmmsg batching

macOS:

  capture   -> ScreenCaptureKit
  encode    -> VideoToolbox
  input     -> CGEvent
  clipboard -> NSPasteboard
  audio     -> ScreenCaptureKit + AAC
  service   -> launchd LaunchAgent

Network:

  signed rendezvous + direct end-to-end encrypted UDP + blind encrypted relay fallback

Rendezvous server

  make rendezvous-server
  make deploy-rendezvous
